#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077} :activity/id #uuid "da7c19c9-a092-5227-8c3d-c60c670a8222" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206 :topic/id #uuid "8850e3a0-f2fb-5671-843a-13422a22aa79" :topic/math-academy-id 4387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199560 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $A$ and $B$ are sets, the Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs $(a,b)$ whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\big\\{(a, b) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, b \\in B \\big\\}\n$$\n\nMany Cartesian products have natural representations in the Cartesian plane. The most common of these is the Cartesian product $\\mathbb R^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^2 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y) \\: : \\: x,y \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis infinite set contains all ordered pairs $(x,y),$ where $x$ and $y$ can be any real numbers. In other words, $\\mathbb R^2$ contains every point in the Cartesian plane! \n\n![](images/07/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png)\n\nSimilarly, the set\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^3 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y, z) \\: : \\: x,y,z \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\ndescribes every point in three-dimensional Cartesian space. \n\n![](images/e7/e74fb5948144bc61173e4de73865cb746278bd31fd87e4abf927fa01821ba936.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c18e6618-573b-5560-8d95-58ed56f05155" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8237 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c7652427-e5ff-5d02-9f23-39039f090aff" :step/math-academy-id 153383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198272 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198169 :question/id #uuid "bb40c174-3739-50df-835e-5d47c9902d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421830}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "2190f1df-5853-5d8c-85e2-33f40f1f0f24" :step/math-academy-id 153384 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199579 :tutorial/content "Cartesian coordinates can also be used to visualize other Cartesian products.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following Cartesian product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^- = \\big\\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in\\mathbb N,\\, y\\in\\mathbb R^-\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis product contains every point in $\\mathbb R^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative real number.\n\nTo visualize this Cartesian product, consider the following subset of our Cartesian product:\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\big\\{ (1,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. We can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f6165f66b4149b7b2ea52a3cd686836853d85048ca890e850cb771174a150c57.png)\n\nNext, we consider the subset $S_2,$ given by\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{ (2,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-.\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. \n\n![](images/ca/caf2299a2e8009ad5b2776ce67786fa7c7e3bdb57aacbd7df87882f59a2350e2.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3, S_4, S_5,\\ldots,$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N\\times {R}^{-} & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup ⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-$ as the set of vertical rays shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/033c40b86bfd5cebf24d34de644f632213207386c2b67c559af7594468599e1e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1626e562-8e88-5ff3-bae3-4162438c0086" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8329 :tutorial/title "Cartesian Products of Countably Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "cd371945-c79b-5b78-9017-15c39e4d5ed7" :step/math-academy-id 153387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198154 :question/id #uuid "eae94514-cd92-5405-90b1-ee09dad18e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050088}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389916}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "48b1fb46-7071-5897-9dd7-a47e1d955f6c" :step/math-academy-id 153388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199584 :tutorial/content "Let's sketch a representation of the set $S= [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane.\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS = [0,1] \\times [0, 1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\n\\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in [0,1],\\, y\\in [0,1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_0 \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}0}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{red}0} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{red}{0}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_{\\color{red}0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general $S_{\\color{blue}{i}} \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{blue}{i}} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{blue}{i}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor each ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1],$ $S_{\\color{blue}{i}}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},0)$ to the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},1).$\n\n![](images/28/28b9aa0559635ed4090f10dd0b59f2cbb6c297f8736d9509a637b5d78d27561c.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I = [0,1]{:}$\n\n$$\nS = \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}.\n$$\n\nAs $\\color{blue}i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}{:}$\n\n![](images/76/76ff48755dbfc3ba1943665d08aa8cfd00948bbc1ddfaab560a0af64f11bae26.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "923fb377-d694-5181-8561-c57612f15054" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8339 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Product [0,1] x [0,1]"} :step/id #uuid "ef01039c-9cce-558a-8067-2ffba087e848" :step/math-academy-id 153392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198205 :question/id #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13320"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390333}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "9ddfd97d-7a1a-59ef-8492-3fbe4088f22f" :step/math-academy-id 153393 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198206 :question/id #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" :question/math-academy-id "e-13321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389797}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "5777ec1b-f050-570f-86e4-1e7bffa77dfb" :step/math-academy-id 153396}] :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198532}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605} :activity/id #uuid "cac9bb11-d48e-5140-9653-cc117881f57c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176968 :tutorial/content "Under what conditions can we be sure that the global extrema of a function exist?\n\nThe **extreme value theorem** answers this question and states the following:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a continuous function defined on the closed interval $[a,b],$ then $f(x)$ always has at least one global maximum and one global minimum on $[a,b]$*.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5].\n$$\n\nNotice that this function satisfies the conditions for the extreme value theorem:\n\n- the function $f(x)$ is *continuous* on the interval $x\\in [2,5]$, and\n\n- the interval $[2,5]$ is *closed*.\n\nSince *both* conditions are satisfied, the extreme value theorem guarantees that $f(x)$ must have at least one global maximum and one global minimum.\n\nTo see that this is indeed true, let's sketch the graph of $f(x).$ \n\n![](images/d2/d2ceffc40ead80b03d73004b663b91a942444bd14ce9e1e68fc98ffebbae3423.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the function $f(x)$ has \n\n- a global maximum at $x=5,$ and\n\n- a global minimum at $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "300a3bb8-a79f-5387-8c5e-1e403c2df531" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2666 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9ff3fa5a-39c6-574d-bfa0-7e467bf44083" :step/math-academy-id 12791 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175768 :question/id #uuid "59426214-0bb1-5e8b-9e96-0fb84ec6361c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342003}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "8c5c661d-87d1-5fdd-b4cf-9b1217993d69" :step/math-academy-id 12792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175902 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175769 :question/id #uuid "2d31d7a1-bd6a-5794-a4af-b6cdad4dc64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341981}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "3001b2d1-c302-5b15-b3a6-3ff0fa36821e" :step/math-academy-id 12794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175770 :question/id #uuid "1d90dec2-3c79-5069-ba1b-7e7e3f16ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341988}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7889a404-286a-5541-b477-ed0966b909de" :step/math-academy-id 12795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177036 :tutorial/content "We can only apply the extreme value theorem to *continuous* functions defined on *closed* intervals. Both conditions are crucial.\n\nTo see why this is the case, consider the function $g(x),$ given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=(x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5).\n$$\n\nNotice that $g(x)$ is defined on a semi-open interval (the right endpoint is open). As a result, we cannot guarantee that $g(x)$ has a global maximum and a global minimum.\n\n![](images/a7/a7648d89fc02372f8d48a649425fa5dba4c8a86e12e9fa7bbddc69ffe52575c3.png)\n\nSimilarly, consider the function $h(x),$ given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}, & \\,2\\le x<3, \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+1, & \\,3\\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ is defined on the closed interval $[2,5].$ However, since $h(x)$ is not continuous on $[2,5],$ we cannot guarantee that $h(x)$ has both a global maximum and minimum on $[2,5].$\n\n![](images/b1/b1bdc0a3c384e5918bdac46ce5db31c0836069eb8a3383febf2aac86654929ea.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "08bf8574-c1f7-5521-983b-282f7b0c3cc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5745 :tutorial/title "Conditions for Applying the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "fa2543e5-fe3a-57fe-a964-118b017f51bc" :step/math-academy-id 35242 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607}}] :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176121}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176855} :activity/id #uuid "0b6911e4-5f07-57c1-b15a-445e4baf17de" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049091 :topic/id #uuid "d9b41e19-22fc-5fad-93ae-b8f50010d70b" :topic/math-academy-id 3557} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177064 :tutorial/content "Let's look again at the graph of the function $y=\\cos x.$ \n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nThe cosine function has infinitely many zeros and an infinite number of points where the function reaches its maximum and minimum value. Let's write general expressions for these properties, assuming $n$ is an integer.\n\n**The Zeros of the Function**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the zeros of the function are $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pm \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\dots$ \n\nA general expression for the zeros is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Maxima of the Function**\n\nThe function has a maximum when $x=0$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the values of $x$ that maximize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Minima of the Function**\n\nThe function has a minimum when $x=\\pi$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the values of $x$ that minimize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = \\pi + 2n\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "46e10740-fb99-59c2-8277-45bf39d4988e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6896 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "96434733-6b0d-57f8-b32a-6d08028990e9" :step/math-academy-id 41780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175693 :question/id #uuid "70569afd-42c4-5e5f-8397-4fbd873eb8e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-11002"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050718}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338800}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"} :step/id #uuid "917ddcd7-bc4f-5e36-8f78-dd85b3ecbfe5" :step/math-academy-id 41781 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177065 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of the cosine function again.\n\n![](images/04/04f13f2dec860874174f51990f4260615e7be893a6be5f86b3b13857c4e8953f.png)\n\nThe periodicity of the function is another characteristic of its graph. The period of the cosine function is $2\\pi,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/78/789fa06939a3e3350a18c4e9cdf078bf6baf3f025709703e608cb3b7ba89e698.png)\n\nThis means that $\\cos\\left( x+2n\\pi\\right) = \\cos \\left( x\\right)$ for any number $x$ and any integer $n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5cc2e21c-1a75-53dd-85be-61d9124f25c6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6897 :tutorial/title "The Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"} :step/id #uuid "95d7359c-3367-567b-8a0b-4546d0d324da" :step/math-academy-id 41784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176858}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176048 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175694 :question/id #uuid "a8cc8b8f-463b-5734-a33d-e915159cba32" :question/math-academy-id "e-11003"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c25c3a0-083a-55d5-b688-7347bfafaa9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049353}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338803}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"} :step/id #uuid "fdae8229-a567-5401-adbc-7dc3c17d7b91" :step/math-academy-id 41785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176895}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176057 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175703 :question/id #uuid "08253dde-510a-5451-ba5c-64d42d647db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11012"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275434}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"} :step/id #uuid "763338f7-5348-5442-87d5-00b41679ebf8" :step/math-academy-id 41829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176895 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177082 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *even* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, cosine is an even function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos(-x) = \\cos(x).\n$$\n\nEven functions are always symmetric about the $y$-axis. This is indeed true of the cosine function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/b1/b1f44126ba6188d147026b6fe5a4066b0051ec3b638bb2d3e34ee1cbb1708cae.png)\n\nWe can use the evenness property of cosine in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7ca8a70-b8fa-5e67-adec-ee263651a473" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7897 :tutorial/title "The Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"} :step/id #uuid "5a441a39-9db7-5be4-8b0d-dbbb978a8599" :step/math-academy-id 45521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176875}}] :activity/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176108}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176655} :activity/id #uuid "275b0afa-011e-599e-83db-08f12becae8b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049126 :topic/id #uuid "dcb0f682-2f5d-57d5-ac9a-10eb5e8a8b36" :topic/math-academy-id 3591} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176950 :tutorial/content "Given two finite data sets of equal length, say,\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\},\n$$\n\nthe **covariance** between $x$ and $y,$ denoted by $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ is a measure that evaluates the extent to which $x$ and $y$ change together.\n\n- A positive covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is also likely to increase:\n\n![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n\n- A negative covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is likely to decrease:\n\n![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\n- A covariance of zero (or close to zero) indicates that there is no *linear* relationship between $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/a5/a58fa4d40d77af23df86b5bd8eaca3119b93bc9ea97cc435eb4e53ba2777b071.png)\n\n**Watch Out!** A covariance of zero does *not* mean that $x$ and $y$ are not related. It simply means the relationship between them is not linear. To illustrate, consider the image below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4bdbf92e691174644dda9b9a84acb1b896c0436afb221501dc10b4635841539.png)\n\nThe (nonlinear) relationship shown above is $y=x^2.$ However, it can be shown that $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = 0$ for this data set.\n\nTo calculate $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ we use the following formula: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$  and $\\overline{y}$ denote the means of $x$ and $y$ respectively.\n\n**Note**: This formula for the covariance assumes that $x$ and $y$ are drawn from a *population*. There is a slightly different formula for covariance when $x$ and $y$ are sample data. However, we won't worry about the difference for now." :tutorial/id #uuid "da03b3e7-b452-59b9-b81e-5dd75cea02a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10301 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a9620c91-7f1f-5c90-81f9-15b63580b9b6" :step/math-academy-id 217080 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175746 :question/id #uuid "ef520918-4e4b-516b-acaa-57a14b5c1405" :question/math-academy-id "e-16134"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050862}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275215}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"} :step/id #uuid "7c94f745-4162-5e7d-841a-0a7d3f00e84a" :step/math-academy-id 217081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175937 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175747 :question/id #uuid "33adfe2b-297e-5c52-8c4f-088d0c2b66e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16136"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275210}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance in Context"} :step/id #uuid "48e20fe8-234e-5200-b8a8-908f518d0da4" :step/math-academy-id 217084 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176951 :tutorial/content "Variance measures the spread of a *single* data set $x$ around its mean value $\\overline{x}.$ On the other hand, covariance measures the relationship between *two* data sets $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe (population) covariance of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad  \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nis given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nIf we compare a single data set, say $x$, against itself using our formula for covariance, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,x) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2$ denotes the variance of $x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "42b9ca98-be0b-50e0-9b90-f3e38732fcd0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10305 :tutorial/title "The Connection Between Variance and Covariance"} :step/id #uuid "2661268b-e076-5805-af10-02ffd1c3fc5e" :step/math-academy-id 217087}] :activity/title "Covariance" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176135}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172765} :activity/id #uuid "5ec40baf-865b-5a44-991d-3e7fe88c0d58" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049243 :topic/id #uuid "e61e45e7-7c85-53b1-babf-321815b4e8ac" :topic/math-academy-id 3404} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171956 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171808 :question/id #uuid "104ad420-9a16-51b9-bfc5-16080b22fd49" :question/math-academy-id "e-5108"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be11193b-a241-5812-a1c1-54d233ae9ddd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335994}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Binomial Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "2865273f-2c72-526a-8ae4-5f9b2067a41a" :step/math-academy-id 18724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172764}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172764 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171957 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171809 :question/id #uuid "587f0594-4911-5ad7-9f1a-bd33cfbad3fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5109"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c976b6a-4523-51e1-a243-27aa8937db25" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335998}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Relationship Between the Rows of Pascal's Triangle in Terms of Binomial Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "1189fea7-65d2-5d2d-87d5-800d8204673c" :step/math-academy-id 18731 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173144 :tutorial/content "Remember that when counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero!\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (on the left) is position number $0.$\n\nWe can write a value in Pascal's triangle as $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{a} \\choose {\\color{blue}b}},$ where the value is located in row $\\color{red}a$ and position ${\\color{blue}b}.$\nThis is known as a **binomial coefficient**.\nIn words, we say $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{a} \\choose {\\color{blue}b}}$ as \"$\\color{red}{a}$ choose ${\\color{blue}b}$\".\n\nFor example, the value located in row $\\color{red}4$ and position $\\color{blue}2$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle{{\\color{red}4} \\choose \\color{blue}{2}} = 6.$ Remember, we start counting from zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "62d4b142-a86a-52c9-b45a-1153afedf865" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3393 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "133af8ac-b2ce-5603-9334-ce3da1e8d068" :step/math-academy-id 18977 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172766 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171958 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171810 :question/id #uuid "9f9efb07-33a0-5a28-8eef-656a3c00a200" :question/math-academy-id "e-5231"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5c108c0-f32a-5725-af3b-c3e747da09f2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049341}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338779}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Binomial Coefficients Using the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "0b7576cb-372f-5a6a-ad5b-4f0b958a39e3" :step/math-academy-id 19269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173146 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wanted to calculate the value of $\\displaystyle{30 \\choose 3}.$ If we were to use Pascal's triangle, we'd have to write out $31$ rows, which would take a long time. Thankfully, there is a formula for calculating binomial coefficients. \n\nThe binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle{n \\choose r}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{n \\choose r} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,  r!}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b89d1a7e-10c1-5ec2-b745-c3c45e0d7814" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3893 :tutorial/title "A Formula for the Binomial Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "b2f96998-bbd7-586b-b0d8-4cdbe2c42034" :step/math-academy-id 23007 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172766}}] :activity/title "Pascal's Triangle and the Binomial Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172193}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230086} :activity/id #uuid "a9d8220e-5eea-558d-97c2-fb85d6eeb928" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370 :topic/id #uuid "ef58e174-0df4-568f-8b8f-ebb5d1dacf42" :topic/math-academy-id 6678} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230198 :tutorial/content "Recall that a linear first-order homogeneous differential equation in the variable $y = y(x)$ takes the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= 0.\n$$\n\nFor example, the equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6xy = 0\n$$\n\nis a linear first-order homogeneous ODE with $a(x) = 1$ and $b(x) = 6x.$ Notice that the right-hand side is identical to zero.\n\nA linear first-order *inhomogeneous* differential equation is similar, except the right-hand side is *not* identical to zero. So, a linear first-order inhomogeneous differential equation can be written as\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= f(x).\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is often called the **forcing function.** We say that a first-order ODE has **polynomial forcing** if $f(x)$ is a polynomial.\n\nFor example, the equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6xy = 2x\n$$\n\nis a linear first-order inhomogeneous ODE with polynomial forcing because the right-hand side, $f(x) = 2x,$ is a nonzero polynomial.\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to find general solutions to linear first-order ODEs with polynomial forcing." :tutorial/id #uuid "3c372faf-ce6c-50ce-ba50-f29bb375063c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14545 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a4508fb6-525e-5090-a9ae-b0755caac38e" :step/math-academy-id 603073 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230175 :tutorial/content "We wish to construct the general solution to the first-order linear inhomogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x) + b(x)y(x) = f(x).\n$$\n\nThere are several ways to find the general solution to a first-order inhomogeneous ODE. The method we'll discuss here is a three-step process that involves finding the sum of the so-called complementary and particular solutions. \n\nThe three steps are as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the **complementary solution** (or the **homogeneous solution**) $y_c(x)$ that satisfies the associated homogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x) + b(x)y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a **particular solution** $y_p(x)$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. This is a single function that satisfies the inhomogeneous equation.\n\n- **Step 3:** Write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution: \n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x).\n$$\n\nAs we'll see, this method generalizes to higher-order linear ODEs, so it's well worth understanding how it works for first-order ODEs.\n\nLet's begin by practicing finding the complementary solution to an equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "eeb7c752-7b59-5c4f-bda0-57bcac65612c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14447 :tutorial/title "A Three-Step Process for Solving First-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "60f91c59-100a-502d-86f9-c9e4e06e6007" :step/math-academy-id 603074 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230226 :tutorial/content "Let's find the complementary solution to the following inhomogeneous ODE:\n![](images/f0/f01ce3a6277c6af1c9c8eb4fc1b217b90b3ca72e33a727674c5a1670ccf51f5c.svg)\n\nTo find the complementary solution ![](images/12/12b17ac320da147720f235a1556bbb9bd404f3c8d100cf3b22d145a293d421d3.svg) we solve the corresponding homogeneous ODE.\nWe start by setting the right-hand side equal to zero:\n![](images/5b/5b189ffb1e490e04dc8aea566914128a88e9c48dcc331ff1f5a32ef4af6ee38c.svg)\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n![](images/a8/a8c8cee52474932eb90446a5d3007eea481549ca3be8de61aa32b0bead87e0e2.svg)\n\nWe separate the variables as follows:\n![](images/a0/a0cc584b2c78bd04361fa9ecc0a539f0466fcd22c9727965ff1b33318c542879.svg)\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to ![](images/06/06d9e6ee04f202e0e861c51f0cd11ce47727f565d64d834fa625baea04ae11e6.svg)\n![](images/b9/b962b410044f1e7be26c3bc15cded89ebc0d32a84d712e0f9fab3a953bc4ce5d.svg)\n\nNext, we make ![](images/02/024420a1f4a1300a242618c4742c03a91e27ee710937bb23acd902722711833c.svg) the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n![](images/ec/ec64cfca27de9b893156b962bcb27bd24af86fe03910cd89dbb48ae3c10d1e6d.svg)\nwhere ![](images/bc/bcee7e8a93dffc0dbf0dbd42566a68b0f25841376c48f286dc9670d635318d80.svg)\n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the constant to be positive or negative (absorbing the sign into the constant).\nTherefore, the complementary solution is\n![](images/c2/c23ac0e71a5c90e57908d7220af6cc26641787e413a5cd2e1bec7a6a975f752d.svg)\nwhere ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is an arbitrary constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "739946c6-4a73-5a29-a453-c3a79f65fe12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14698 :tutorial/title "Finding a Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "d22c40eb-8457-5361-a39f-e09a1e967559" :step/math-academy-id 603075 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229184 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228892 :question/id #uuid "cd54adb4-ef2b-5f9d-b920-4ec460bcd199" :question/math-academy-id "e-21611"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363732}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"} :step/id #uuid "0cb4876d-bad8-5565-9a50-5f0d4944b541" :step/math-academy-id 603076 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230179 :tutorial/content "We've discussed how to find the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ of a first-order inhomogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= f(x).\n$$\n\nWe will now turn our attention to finding a **particular solution.**\n\nThe particular solution $y_p(x)$ of a linear ODE captures the part of the solution that arises directly from the forcing term $f(x).$\nIn other words, if we substitute $y_p(x)$ into the left-hand side of the equation, it should produce the forcing function $f(x).$\n\nFor equations with constant coefficients, we can often find $y_p(x)$ by looking at the type of function appearing on the right-hand side and then guessing a form that matches it. This is sometimes called **the method of undetermined coefficients.**\n\nFor example, if the forcing function $f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $n,$ we guess a particular solution that is also a polynomial of degree $n.$  After making this guess, we substitute it into the differential equation and solve for the unknown constants.\n\nNext, we'll work through a concrete example where $f(x)$ is a linear polynomial." :tutorial/id #uuid "c9c34b5c-1c79-52c1-8f45-ab3604573871" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14457 :tutorial/title "Finding a Particular Solution"} :step/id #uuid "d041084b-42d5-550f-9c4b-326eaf35adbd" :step/math-academy-id 603080 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230237 :tutorial/content "Consider the inhomogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 5 y = 5x-16.\n$$\n\nHere, the forcing term\n\n$$\nf(x) = 5x - 16\n$$\n\nis a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ so we guess\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nNow substitute $y_p$ and $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha - 5(\\alpha x + \\beta ) = 5x-16.\n$$\n\nNext, group the terms proportional to $x$ and the constants:\n\n$$\n-5\\alpha x + (\\alpha - 5\\beta) = 5x-16.\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients on both sides gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -5\\beta =-16\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-1$ and $\\beta=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  -x+3.\n$$\n\nLet's see another example where the forcing function is a quadratic polynomial." :tutorial/id #uuid "40a1be07-f998-5d91-9693-ba72cd0662fb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14798 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "6635843c-8657-56a7-b649-c24365b05e2b" :step/math-academy-id 603081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229185 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228893 :question/id #uuid "e0e14ce3-61de-571e-b34e-f56630cc2fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21612"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409496}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "a4d952f1-49ab-54a9-a0d5-a77f50eb7f6e" :step/math-academy-id 603082 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230180 :tutorial/content "Now that we've practiced finding both the complementary and particular solutions, let's put them together to find the **general solution**.\n\nRecall that the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ comes from solving the associated homogeneous equation. It contains an arbitrary constant, so it represents all homogeneous solutions.\n\nThe particular solution $y_p(x)$ is one specific function that satisfies the full inhomogeneous equation.\n\nTo find the general solution, we add these two pieces:\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x).\n$$\n\nThe particular solution already satisfies the full equation.\nAdding any complementary solution preserves this property while introducing the arbitrary constant needed to match different initial conditions.\n\nIn this way, the formula above describes *every possible solution* of a linear inhomogeneous equation.\n\nLet's consider an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "fa89e8c5-100d-5467-abfe-c8b867a6de87" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14458 :tutorial/title "Constructing the General Solution"} :step/id #uuid "636066af-6797-59c7-b358-5bb8f6e14683" :step/math-academy-id 603086 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230238 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = 16.\n$$\n\nWe will find its general solution by computing the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n3 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using separation of variables gives\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{-2/3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we will find a particular solution. To find a particular solution of\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = 16,\n$$\n\nnote that the forcing term is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nTherefore, we assume the particular solution is also a constant:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\beta.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y_p$ and its derivative into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 0 + 2\\beta = 16.\n$$\n\nSolving yields $\\beta = 8,$ so\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 8.\n$$\n\nFinally, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x) = Ae^{-2/3x} + 8.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fd25308f-b077-5d29-98f3-6c1ad2c59cb1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14799 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "f36fce91-06d8-5fe4-a8b5-076c5ddf85e8" :step/math-academy-id 603087 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230095}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229186 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228894 :question/id #uuid "344fb3a9-81db-5744-822c-1f989c0cc4de" :question/math-academy-id "e-21613"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409495}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "763320a8-8e20-5ddf-9411-3580fafb3b1f" :step/math-academy-id 603088 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229187 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228895 :question/id #uuid "f89b7459-5b3d-5da3-a76a-7df3378cb048" :question/math-academy-id "e-21614"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365630}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "0a4bd77e-83fd-5e30-a4b6-14501cd22c8a" :step/math-academy-id 603092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230199 :tutorial/content "Consider the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\na(x)y' + b(x)y = f(x).\n$$\n\nWe'll now prove that the sum of the complementary and particular solutions gives a general solution to the equation. \n\nFirst, we note that, by definition, the complementary solution $y_c$ satisfies\n\n$$\na(x)y'_c + b(x)y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nThis can be solved by separating the variables, and the solution is given by (for $a(x)\\neq 0$)\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\exp\\left(-\\int \\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}\\,\\textrm d x\\right).\n$$\n\nBy definition, the particular solution satisfies\n\n$$\na(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p = f(x).\n$$\n\nWe claim that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the most general solution to the equation. \n\n- First, we show that $y_c + y_p$ satisfies the equation. Substituting this into the left-hand side of the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x){y}^{'}+b(x)y & =a(x)({y}_{c}^{'}+{y}_{p}^{'})+b(x)({y}_{c}+{y}_{p}) \\\\  & ={{[a(x){y}_{c}^{'}+b(x){y}_{c}]}_{⏟}}_{0}+[a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p}] \\\\  & =a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, suppose that $y$ is *any* solution to the equation, and $y_p$ is a known, particular solution. Substituting $y - y_p$ into the left-hand side of the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x)(y-{y}_{p})^{'}+b(x)(y-{y}_{p}) & =a(x)({y}^{'}-{y}_{p}^{'})+b(x)(y-{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =[a(x){y}^{'}+b(x)y]-[a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y$ is a solution, we have $a(x)y' + b(x)y = f(x)$, and since $y_p$ is a particular solution, we have $a(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p = f(x)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\left[a(x)y' + b(x)y\\right] - \\left[a(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p\\right]\n= f(x) - f(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, $y - y_p$ satisfies the homogeneous equation, and hence $y - y_p = y_c$. This implies that any solution $y$ is the sum of $y_c$ and $y_p.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7c1dca3c-5c18-5c60-a4e4-062b1da7f872" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14549 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Form of the General Solution"} :step/id #uuid "d734be5c-d8f2-5ce1-99a8-b3ef88363c6f" :step/math-academy-id 603096 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230236 :tutorial/content "Technically, a particular solution $y_p$ is *not unique*.\n\nIf $y_p$ solves\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)\\,y = Q(x),\n$$\n\nand $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)\\,y = 0,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\ny_c + y_p\n$$\n\nalso solves the inhomogeneous equation, which means that $y_c + y_p$ is also a particular solution!\n\nHowever, in practice, even though $y_p$ is not unique, it’s very common to use the phrase **“the particular solution”** to mean \"a particular solution that *does not include* any terms from the complementary (homogeneous) solution\".\n\nSo, we typically choose $y_p$ to avoid overlapping with $y_c$, since the full solution is written as\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p.\n$$\n\nThis makes the roles of $y_c$ and $y_p$ clean and non-redundant." :tutorial/id #uuid "c8acee86-4e0b-5fcf-bd6d-8e401a83e24f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14779 :tutorial/title "A Common Convention for “the” Particular Solution"} :step/id #uuid "c87f1941-5836-59f4-967c-4667e6ea5915" :step/math-academy-id 603097}] :activity/title "General Solutions of First-Order Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229249}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409500} :activity/id #uuid "0aa93e49-72e3-5515-b5a3-3f4b53ceca51" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370 :topic/id #uuid "ef58e174-0df4-568f-8b8f-ebb5d1dacf42" :topic/math-academy-id 6678} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447d61bd-85be-5a77-92c7-e7b1cbcac24d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{e}^{-4x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409486} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e554faf-bf14-5f8b-8095-42b409f87077" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "927f3245-f67b-58d6-852d-475c4b37e55f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332544" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-8.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+4\\beta =-8\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}-2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "19ee83f6-1de2-5260-a0ac-12e506110b20" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409498}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4099c08f-db10-5b94-b476-722ec92e2f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305785} {:answer/id #uuid "424f3742-f4f4-58bf-9771-5a09636ce05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305805} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea666d9-fb1e-528b-9c1a-2a72695635a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306503} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e7bafe-af2c-5d92-a1f9-afdc8f293f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306732} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a9087d-0776-582b-a88f-17e01518a957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306856}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305805} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a523e35-796d-5af3-b102-9fe77f79d844" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "da39297b-062f-5af6-a7fc-07a4dc407cf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-333042" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-4(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -4\\beta )x+(\\beta -4\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =-2\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -4\\beta =5\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta -4\\gamma =-3\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},$ $\\beta =-1,$ and $\\gamma =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4(0)}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}-(0)+\\frac{1}{2} & =-1 \\\\ A+\\frac{1}{2} & =-1 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "218a75cb-ded3-56d6-8e5a-f6b0268721ec"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a813be27-9e89-5447-a6ed-ccb753aec0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Ae^{-6x}+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409488} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea7da04c-6c47-53af-93ba-6b58aa8bcba5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4bc0ce6c-d00c-58b5-b1e7-41739599c291" :question/math-academy-id "q-332541" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=12x+8.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x+8\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(\\alpha +6\\beta )=12x+8\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +6\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x}+2x+1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c1d4d31-bdd3-5e06-8ac4-128b5af0e137" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409497}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79ad64d0-e59a-5622-ad8f-a7d521d9e58a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409487} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c92c86-227c-5c65-bc14-31ff63640148" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "abc3945b-d976-54fe-bc38-a631f9c8a6c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332291" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=8.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha =8\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5fac2716-ce9b-5cad-acde-41963ce110a0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcf2db36-4823-5f0f-a979-18eb641ef853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409489} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc1fdf7c-1bca-53aa-87e0-8b914a783aff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11008462-bb8d-5c30-9ab6-4b789c8f149b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332303" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=12x-8.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x-8\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(\\alpha -6\\beta )=12x-8.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -6\\beta =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e7fb33d5-37b5-560d-9083-478d43405b96" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409499}}] :activity/title "General Solutions of First-Order Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409485}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176863} :activity/id #uuid "f9ca5fe5-6853-50b1-a844-63b8f9239024" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050359 :topic/id #uuid "424e5d7e-179f-525e-bdaa-70125807f572" :topic/math-academy-id 3563} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176863 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177068 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\sec x$ once again.\n\n![](images/da/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nThe graph has infinitely many asymptotes, and there is an infinite number of points where the function takes values of $1$ and $-1.$ We can write down general expressions for these properties, by assuming $n$ represents an integer.\n\n**The Asymptotes of the Function**\n\nThe function has asymptotes when $x=\\pm \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pm \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\ldots,$ etc. Therefore, a general equation for the asymptotes of the graph is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Points Where the Function Takes a Value of $\\mathbf{1}$**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function takes a value of $1$ when $x=0.$ Since the function has a period of $2\\pi,$ a general expression for the values of $x$ for which $\\sec x = 1$ is\n\n$$\nx = 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Points Where the Function Takes a Value of $\\mathbf{-1}$**\n\nSimilarly, from the graph, we see that the function takes a value of $-1$ when $x=\\pi.$ Since the function has a period $2\\pi,$ a general expression for the values of $x$ for which $\\sec x = -1$ is\n\n$$\nx = \\pi +2n\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ffb14f55-8ddb-57b9-92f1-eb23d2746f3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6900 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dad82092-08fe-5e6b-a790-190be930d3ac" :step/math-academy-id 41798 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176864}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176864 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176051 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175697 :question/id #uuid "06828f66-a8be-59f5-b82d-afd93333f38d" :question/math-academy-id "e-11006"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049091}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335827}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"} :step/id #uuid "b2bd5088-bb7b-573e-af6d-29ccdbbd3846" :step/math-academy-id 41799 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176865}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176865 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177069 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the graph of the function $y=\\sec x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/da/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nAs we've stated before, the secant function inherits its periodicity from the cosine function, so the period is $2\\pi.$\n\n![](images/ff/ffa196e0ad8b61e3ec113d24ac976c184b5520d271a739ca1b74fc05d4340619.png)\n\nThe periodicity of $y=\\sec x$ means that\n\n$$\n\\sec (x+2n\\pi ) = \\sec x\n$$\n\nfor any integer $n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "941c3765-23e3-5c9e-9a00-88956e1a33c2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6901 :tutorial/title "The Periodicity Property of the Secant Function"} :step/id #uuid "07c7109d-2060-5e6d-a554-c5e3ab9de0ff" :step/math-academy-id 41802 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176866}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176866 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176052 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175698 :question/id #uuid "f76b3e8f-5580-5cb0-8d6c-85711c446602" :question/math-academy-id "e-11007"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049353}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338794}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function"} :step/id #uuid "2ec04d1a-a778-5f20-8d35-4f86acb404bd" :step/math-academy-id 41803 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176059 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175705 :question/id #uuid "6bd67fa4-16b5-5505-bfc5-35a00f54e980" :question/math-academy-id "e-11014"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b378b0-30e5-59a8-b9f7-882b5e226a2e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275431}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Secant Function"} :step/id #uuid "6076f40f-9346-5c9e-82d5-e03ff68f0f85" :step/math-academy-id 41837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177085 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *even* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, secant is an even function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec(-x) = \\sec(x).\n$$\n\nEven functions are always symmetric about the $y$-axis. This is indeed true of the secant function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/07/07caca56100a2a6ea989f6a0d1efd6227bf177ba559678091926b9462aefa054.png)\n\nWe can use the evenness property of secant in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a2a9752b-088c-5b8f-b110-2194d3ba44b5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7901 :tutorial/title "The Evenness Property of the Secant Function"} :step/id #uuid "3dbc8e15-1668-5f2d-bb27-871e6077967b" :step/math-academy-id 45526 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176877}}] :activity/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176114}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176871} :activity/id #uuid "c0c3ea68-0fd9-5638-b616-f302c26374a0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050427 :topic/id #uuid "48167c86-51da-525c-908a-de3945756954" :topic/math-academy-id 3566} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177072 :tutorial/content "Recall the graph of the function $y=\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/e0/e01402141f79b247b05980f7f4e830c112a7dcdc24232a8a4c9beefc1de6f603.png)\n\nSimilarly to the tangent, the cotangent has infinitely many zeros, and the function has an infinite number of vertical asymptotes.\nLet's construct general expressions for these, assuming that $n$ represents an integer.\n\n**The Zeros of the Function**\n\nFrom the graph, the function has a zero when $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2,$ and the function is periodic with period $\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the zeros is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+ n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Vertical Asymptotes of the Function**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the vertical asymptotes of the function occur at $x=0,\\pm\\pi,\\pm2\\pi,\\dots.$\n\nTherefore, a general equation for the asymptotes is\n\n$$\nx = n\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "82b3983d-7311-5ed6-9dc8-b6249cce7344" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6904 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35087e42-c157-511c-b754-837cf1f03ab6" :step/math-academy-id 41814 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176055 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175701 :question/id #uuid "b934301a-aa9f-5ed6-9a31-e05391d1913c" :question/math-academy-id "e-11010"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a18aadb-3e8a-54d7-a5d7-e78208e77675" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050523}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338801}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cotangent Function"} :step/id #uuid "fd7ff042-6ded-5c03-8aaa-84bacb531257" :step/math-academy-id 41815 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176873}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177073 :tutorial/content "Let's inspect the graph of the function $y=\\cot x$ one more time.\n\n![](images/e0/e01402141f79b247b05980f7f4e830c112a7dcdc24232a8a4c9beefc1de6f603.png)\n\nWe've seen that the function $y=\\cot x$ is periodic, where the period is $\\pi.$\n\n![](images/1e/1e2477811a1fbf82fed27dfa51e28b7116e2e255e71739b71606526656727559.png)\n\nThe periodicity of $y=\\cot x$ means that $\\cot\\left( x+n\\pi\\right) = \\cot\\left( x\\right)$ for any real number $x$ and for any integer $n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60b36aaa-d3d6-52d6-92ab-8446cdbaec05" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6905 :tutorial/title "The Periodicity Property of the Cotangent Function"} :step/id #uuid "1613a264-e42e-568f-8ee0-9bceb18803be" :step/math-academy-id 41818 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176874}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176056 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175702 :question/id #uuid "d4ebf4f8-d45a-5657-9331-1a6867110288" :question/math-academy-id "e-11011"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02418c3-4c04-57b8-8f10-18858b1759ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049353}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338804}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cotangent Function"} :step/id #uuid "15a54bd7-9049-5e1a-ad7e-274ac04c0387" :step/math-academy-id 41819 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176897}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176061 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175707 :question/id #uuid "2c8233f9-6749-5244-a209-6bf41b64388f" :question/math-academy-id "e-11016"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0251566-4dfc-5786-b00a-ce609e5b2698" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275436}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cotangent Function"} :step/id #uuid "ec3c6030-bffa-5eaf-85f1-d3ce40e9b682" :step/math-academy-id 41845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177084 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, cotangent is an odd function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cot(-x) = -\\cot(x).\n$$\n\nOdd functions always have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This is indeed true of the cotangent function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/02/026258aaa510ef7c61d536a7ff1d1e2001e659663976fa7c9baaccb43f13d83f.png)\n\nWe can use the oddness property of cotangent in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9e59ad18-8aba-541f-9099-4d534d4ac17d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7900 :tutorial/title "The Oddness Property of the Cotangent Function"} :step/id #uuid "73f6a4db-34c0-555b-a3a5-67d15719e66d" :step/math-academy-id 45525 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176879}}] :activity/title "Describing Properties of the Cotangent Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176116}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176859} :activity/id #uuid "5f43bfbe-a51c-5be9-8b78-730c73414b85" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050523 :topic/id #uuid "5049bff8-1875-5713-9a67-c3aa9836de2c" :topic/math-academy-id 3558} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177066 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nThis graph also has infinitely many zeros, and there is an infinite number of asymptotes of the function. We can write down general expressions for these properties, assuming $n$ represents an integer.\n\n**The Zeros of the Function**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the zeros of the function are $x=0,\\pm\\pi,\\dots.$ \n\nWe write down a general expression for the zeros as follows:\n\n$$\nx = n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Asymptotes of the Function**\n\nThe function has an asymptote when $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ and the function is periodic with period $\\pi.$ Therefore, a general equation for the asymptotes of the graph is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "111ba054-af7d-5be0-af1d-1080fc6071e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6898 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7dfd0493-9dae-5c24-8d37-dd6c086dbe60" :step/math-academy-id 41789 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175695 :question/id #uuid "2f4571af-99cf-5a4a-9998-fb6a0f7d6be9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11004"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049155}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297238}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"} :step/id #uuid "4857d80a-89f3-5b48-b3db-fecae223dd5c" :step/math-academy-id 41791 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177067 :tutorial/content "Recall again the graph of the function $y=\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nWe've seen that the function $y=\\tan x$ is periodic, where the period is $\\pi.$\n\n![](images/e9/e911ee6c5bf6c178812c3079633544653066eb6371c4e6c38e3d2df51e622742.png)\n\n   The periodicity of $y=\\tan x$ means that $\\tan\\left( x+n\\pi\\right) = \\tan\\left( x\\right)$ for any number $x$ and any integer $n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "122729e9-d91f-504c-b522-31198dacc035" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6899 :tutorial/title "The Periodicity Property of the Tangent Function"} :step/id #uuid "afb35e22-a15a-5763-9e9e-a1f9cb598b43" :step/math-academy-id 41794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176862}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176862 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176050 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175696 :question/id #uuid "f4649856-d777-5468-a7f4-df50479e36d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11005"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eed0c7c3-0bb5-56eb-a9ca-9ec4dc7b3efe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049353}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338870}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Tangent Function"} :step/id #uuid "cbb21ce8-0d47-5415-989e-adec374bf81b" :step/math-academy-id 41795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176896}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176058 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175704 :question/id #uuid "0b146642-914c-52ce-9939-86752e95dcd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11013"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66a1733a-d7bb-5c86-a154-9e54422053a7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275427}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Tangent Function"} :step/id #uuid "7a0c9edd-c49e-5f53-ae5d-897e790a8db3" :step/math-academy-id 41833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177083 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, tangent is an odd function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nOdd functions always have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This is indeed true of the tangent function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/fa/fa332c1b04ea9ce36e09fae4368d7964d21d3a36cadb115ea2e1e89518af543e.png)\n\nWe can use the oddness property of tangent in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "20305a80-d387-5c47-abec-cd04f79eef76" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7899 :tutorial/title "The Oddness Property of the Tangent Function"} :step/id #uuid "1ab5b11a-1a14-545a-aa8c-8d03af308ee5" :step/math-academy-id 45524 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176876}}] :activity/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176109}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167762} :activity/id #uuid "3c9e36db-811c-5e22-ab47-cb6642c31eaf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050757 :topic/id #uuid "643ac99b-01f2-533f-b1bc-9babbc0cef82" :topic/math-academy-id 3284} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168075 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a sequence of Bernoulli trials, each resulting in success or failure, and with a probability of success $p.$ Let the random variable $X$ represent the number of Bernoulli trials *until the first success*.\n\nIt can be shown that $X$ follows a **geometric distribution** and has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p,\\, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- The factor $(1-p)^{x-1}$ represents the probability of getting exactly $(x-1)$ failures, each with probability $(1-p).$\n\n- The factor ${\\color{purple}{p}}$ represents the probability of getting a single success.\n\n- The condition $x=1,2,3,\\ldots$ indicates that the sequence of Bernoulli trials terminates after a minimum of $1$ trial, and there is no maximum number of trials.\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a geometric distribution, we say it is a geometric random variable and  write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fdc5cdcc-48e5-5c68-a8b0-d4a179a4f320" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5055 :tutorial/title "The Probability Mass Function of a Geometric Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "1d4c3df6-592f-5405-bb8e-5da92b770183" :step/math-academy-id 31642 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166665 :question/id #uuid "e3df676d-e1cc-584e-8b14-a6191a8d6831" :question/math-academy-id "e-8553"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d83ab99-0e27-5989-9a57-6b4a192fa4a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360757}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "bea180fa-6a68-5aff-b1e1-a8d6a9c237fe" :step/math-academy-id 31788 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167668}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166666 :question/id #uuid "f795e566-5cdf-5970-bf94-5b1c838b69f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8554"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120381af-c472-5894-8b19-da2ab61d3a7e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360758}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "eb83b5c2-40bc-5557-850a-406d7b909d09" :step/math-academy-id 31789 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166667 :question/id #uuid "c23e8b4a-dad2-5c6a-95ee-2a439343ded0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8555"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c566525f-9b0f-586f-ba60-277400cb25dd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360760}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Not Zero"} :step/id #uuid "ec4d9548-3439-5b0b-a9f4-7e116d92445d" :step/math-academy-id 31790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166830 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166668 :question/id #uuid "c798d6ea-a258-5a73-8cd2-366e1b3e9db0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8556"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "215130d1-96ea-5f88-8de0-d73758ea8344" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049642}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360764}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "563feb69-65ee-57de-a416-0f33d12b88c6" :step/math-academy-id 31791 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167721}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168108 :tutorial/content "You might be wondering where the geometric distribution gets its name. The answer is that the probability mass function forms a geometric sequence! \n\nSuppose that $X\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p).$ Then, the probability mass function for this geometric distribution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-p)^{x-1}p \\\\ f(x) & =p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\ {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{x}} & ={{p}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}\\cdot ({{1-p}_{⏟}}_{r})^{x-1} \\\\ {a}_{x} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we see that the probability mass function can be interpreted as a geometric sequence where the first term is $a_1=p,$ and the common ratio is $r=1-p.$\n\nMoreover, notice that $f(x)$ satisfies all the criteria to be a probability mass function, namely\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nCriterion 1 is clearly true because $0 \\leq p \\leq 1.$ \n\nTo show that criterion 2 is true, we simply need to calculate the sum of this infinite geometric series, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x) & ={\\sum }_{x=1}^{\\infty }p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{1-(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{p} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is indeed a probability distribution." :tutorial/id #uuid "0ac9b8c4-e381-5d24-b7f2-6e0cf660a14a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5365 :tutorial/title "Showing That a Geometric Distribution Is a Probability Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "4eb95a11-d9d7-553d-a1ee-58106336ccf8" :step/math-academy-id 33495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168131 :tutorial/content "A **Bernoulli trial** is a random experiment whose outcome can be interpreted as either \"success\" or \"failure.\" \n\nFor example, suppose we throw a die and define a \"success\" as the event of getting a six and a \"failure\" as getting any other number. Then, the throw of a die can be thought of as a Bernoulli trial with the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{six}) = \\dfrac16, \\qquad P(\\textrm{not six}) = \\dfrac56\n$$\n\nNow suppose that a fair die is repeatedly thrown *until* we get a six. What is the probability that we need to throw the die precisely ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ times to get our first six?\n\nFirst, notice that each throw of the die is a Bernoulli trial. And since we throw the die repeatedly until we get a six, we have a *sequence* of Bernoulli trials. \n\nLet $X$ be the number of times the die is thrown until we get a six. We wish to compute $P(X = {\\color{blue}{3}}).$ \n\nTo get our first six on the *third* throw, we need to throw \"not a six\" on the *first two* throws, followed by a \"six\" on the *final* throw. Assuming that each throw of the die is independent, by the multiplication law for independent events, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =P(\\text{not six})\\cdot P(\\text{not six})\\cdot P(\\text{six}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{216}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe random variable $X$ in this example follows a so-called **geometric distribution**. Let's now discuss geometric distributions a little more generally." :tutorial/id #uuid "3201acc8-7bbc-59e6-9a50-f9c5f19899e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6168 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8ed0465c-f38c-56a2-acbf-cf8b05298867" :step/math-academy-id 37401 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168132 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ be the number of times a fair die is thrown until we get a six. Since we have a sequence of Bernoulli trials, each with a probability of success $p=\\dfrac16,$ the number of throws until the first six can be modeled as a geometric random variable, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac16\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting our first six on the $\\color{blue}3$rd roll is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{1}{6})}^{3-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{216}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with our previous result." :tutorial/id #uuid "95415f67-f3f8-5c48-9178-812ddf3830e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6169 :tutorial/title "Applying the Probability Mass Function of a Geometric Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "0fec3647-5057-5207-8619-9dd7001d1b85" :step/math-academy-id 37402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167667}}] :activity/title "The Geometric Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167045}]}
